A person is found to have a number of identical signs or posters, all printed from the same original. Each of these signs or posters can be used as evidence of the content of the others, but none of them can be used as evidence of the content of the original.
58. Other evidence includes—
(i) certified copies given according to specific laws;
(ii) copies made from the original using mechanical processes that guarantee accuracy, and copies compared with such copies;
(iii) copies made from the original or compared with the original;
(iv) duplicates of documents against parties who did not sign them;
(v) spoken descriptions of the content of a document by a person who has seen it;
(vi) verbal admissions;
(vii) written admissions;
(viii) testimony of a person who has examined a document made up of numerous accounts or other documents that cannot be easily examined in court, and is an expert in examining such documents.
Examples.
(a) A photo of an original is other evidence of its contents, even if the two have not been compared, if it is proven that the photo was of the original.
(b) A copy compared with a copy of a letter made by a copying machine is other evidence of the contents of the letter, if it is shown that the copy made by the copying machine was from the original.
(c) A copy transcribed from another copy, but later compared with the original, is other evidence; but the copy not compared is not other evidence of the original, even if the original copy was compared with the original.
(d) Neither a spoken description of a copy compared with the original, nor a spoken description of a photo or machine-copy of the original, is other evidence of the original.

Banking Regulation Act, 1949

Act to apply to Co-operative Societies with modifications

The provisions of this Act, as in force for the time being, shall apply to, or in relation to, co-operative societies as they apply to, or in relation to, banking companies subject to certain modifications, namely:
(a) Throughout this Act, unless the context otherwise requires:
(i) references to a "banking company" or "the company" or "such company" shall be construed as references to a Co-operative Bank,
(ii) references to "commencement of this Act" shall be construed as reference to commencement of the Banking Laws (Application to Co-operative Societies) Act, 1965;
(b) In section 2, the words and figures "the Companies Act, 1956, and" shall be omitted;
(c) Certain terms are defined, including "Co-operative Bank," "co-operative credit society," "Director," "primary agricultural credit society," "primary Co-operative Bank," and "primary credit society";
(d) The Act shall override bye-laws, agreements, or resolutions of co-operative societies if they are found to be inconsistent with the Act;
(e) The Act specifies requirements regarding minimum paid-up capital and reserves for Co-operative Banks;
(f) There are restrictions on holding shares in other co-operative societies by Co-operative Banks;
(g) Provisions related to loans, advances, and the use of certain words such as "bank" or "banking" by co-operative societies are outlined in the Act;
(h) Co-operative Banks are required to maintain cash reserve and submit regular accounts and balance-sheets to regulatory authorities;
(i) Provisions related to inspections, exemptions, and actions that the Reserve Bank may take on non-compliance by Co-operative Banks are also included in the Act.

Banking Regulation Act, 1949 applies to Co-operative Banks with modifications as mentioned above.

